WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.575
Version history gives you named checkpoints and ways to
compare them with the current working copy.

00:00:06.695 --> 00:00:11.445
A checkpoint is useful before a risky edit, a review or a
handover.

00:00:11.565 --> 00:00:19.840
We will name a version, change a duration, compare the
result as source and as a drawing, export a review report,
and recover a known state.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.910
History is useful evidence, but it still needs a
deliberate retention and saving routine.

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:32.208
Open File and Version history.

00:00:32.328 --> 00:00:36.878
Enter Before review as the new version name and create the
version.

00:00:36.998 --> 00:00:43.848
Use names that describe a decision or a state, rather than
several indistinguishable copies called final.

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:47.843
Close history and continue editing the working copy.

00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:53.688
Creating a checkpoint gives you a comparison point; it
does not prevent later changes.

00:00:53.808 --> 00:00:58.958
Save the portable document when you want to preserve its
retained history in a file.

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:07.217
Start from a named checkpoint and change Design from five
days to seven.

00:01:07.337 --> 00:01:10.762
Reopen history and select Before review.

00:01:10.882 --> 00:01:14.832
Compare it with the current working copy, then choose
Source.

00:01:14.952 --> 00:01:18.877
Read the changed statement and its surrounding context.

00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:25.497
A text difference tells you exactly what changed,
including edits that may have little visual effect.

00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:32.892
Use it when a semantic summary is incomplete or when
specialized Plant UML syntax needs careful inspection.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.300
Switch to Rendered to inspect the visual consequence of
the same source change.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.570
Check task endpoints, dependencies and nearby work rather
than judging only the changed bar.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:52.740
A rendered comparison helps a reviewer connect the edit to
what people will see.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.260
Return to Source when a highlight is ambiguous.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:03.830
The two comparisons support each other: the picture
explains the effect, while the source identifies the
authored statement.

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:11.608
Choose Review for a semantic summary of recognized
changes.

00:02:11.728 --> 00:02:16.828
Confirmed groups can be selected together and reviewed as
a combined result.

00:02:16.948 --> 00:02:21.148
Related changes may need to stay together to remain valid.

00:02:21.268 --> 00:02:27.718
Unclassified changes require source review, and selecting
a group is not the same as applying it.

00:02:27.838 --> 00:02:34.438
Check the proposed result and any replacement warning
before accepting selected groups into the working copy.

00:02:34.558 --> 00:02:39.458
If the working copy changes during review, review again
before applying.

00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:48.342
A review report records the comparison for someone who
needs to inspect the changes.

00:02:48.462 --> 00:02:53.937
From Review, export the report and keep it alongside the
document for this exercise.

00:02:54.057 --> 00:03:02.307
A selected patch is a more focused artifact containing
selected changes; select and validate those groups before
exporting it.

00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:06.152
Neither artifact replaces the portable project.

00:03:06.272 --> 00:03:13.972
Explain the baseline, the compared working copy and the
intended scope so the recipient knows what the evidence
describes.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:24.425
Create a named review base, then change both the Design
duration and the title in this disposable copy.

00:03:24.545 --> 00:03:30.270
In Review, select only the confirmed duration group and
export its selected patch.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:38.940
Read the replacement warning carefully: applying this
selection from the review base removes unselected
differences from the working copy.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.385
Confirm that consequence only for this exercise, then
apply the selected group.

00:03:44.505 --> 00:03:50.555
Verify that Design remains seven days while the unselected
title change is removed.

00:03:50.675 --> 00:03:56.025
This is why selection, validation and replacement
confirmation belong together.

00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:04.133
Save a connected document before creating the comparison
baseline.

00:04:04.253 --> 00:04:10.553
Change the Requirements label in the work breakdown
structure, then open the document navigator review.

00:04:10.673 --> 00:04:16.173
Inspect which member diagrams and connections differ from
the last successful save.

00:04:16.293 --> 00:04:23.718
A document review gives the connected context, while a
single diagram history comparison answers a narrower
question.

00:04:23.838 --> 00:04:29.038
Export the review report and keep it alongside the
portable practice document.

00:04:29.158 --> 00:04:33.983
Saving a new baseline is a deliberate next step after
reviewing the change.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.350
Select a checkpoint and inspect its name, pin and baseline
controls.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.845
Rename a checkpoint when a clearer description will help
the next reviewer.

00:04:46.965 --> 00:04:52.790
Pin a state you need to retain, and choose a baseline
deliberately for comparisons that use it.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:57.585
Retention still depends on document settings and the
history budget.

00:04:57.705 --> 00:05:06.955
Browser recovery and a saved portable document are
different safeguards, so keep a saved copy before major
experiments or clearing browser data.

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:14.967
Now restore a known checkpoint in this disposable practice
document.

00:05:15.087 --> 00:05:21.387
Start with Before review, change Design to seven days, and
select the earlier checkpoint in history.

00:05:21.507 --> 00:05:25.482
Choose Restore this version and read any confirmation.

00:05:25.602 --> 00:05:31.727
Return to the source and verify that Design is five days
again, then inspect the current preview.

00:05:31.847 --> 00:05:39.047
A restore is an authored change to the working copy, so
save the recovered result when it is the state you intend
to keep.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:45.342
Pause and save a named checkpoint.

00:05:45.462 --> 00:05:51.912
Change a task duration and one label, then compare the
working copy using Source, Rendered and Review.

00:05:52.032 --> 00:05:54.432
Export a review report.

00:05:54.552 --> 00:06:02.102
Locate the checkpoint you would restore, read the
confirmation carefully, and restore it in your disposable
practice document.

00:06:02.222 --> 00:06:06.572
Verify the recovered source and drawing, then save the
result.

00:06:06.692 --> 00:06:12.042
Success means you can explain both what changed and
exactly which state you recovered.

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:22.092
A useful review has a clear starting state, a clear
proposed result and evidence that connects the two.

00:06:22.212 --> 00:06:27.687
Use named checkpoints, complementary comparison views and
explicit acceptance.

00:06:27.807 --> 00:06:32.757
Preserve the portable document and review evidence when
handing work over.

00:06:32.877 --> 00:06:42.952
The next lesson extends this discipline to concurrent
collaboration, where permissions, room lifetime and the
difference between shared work and local history also
matter.
